# Folder Structure Preferences

- Prefer page-owned structure. Logic, components, constants, and services that are used only inside one page should live inside that page feature folder.
- Do not move page-specific code into global folders just because it might look reusable later. Promote code to `src/components`, `src/lib`, or `src/services` only after reuse is real and clear.
- Within each page feature, split by role in detail:

```text
features/<page>/
├── <PageName>Page.tsx
├── <PageName>Page.styled.ts
├── components/
│   └── <component>/
│       ├── <Component>.tsx
│       ├── <Component>.styled.ts
│       └── constants/          # optional, component-only constants
├── constants/                  # page-level copy/data/config
├── services/                   # page-level API or side-effect logic
├── hooks/                      # page-level hooks
├── utils/                      # page-level pure helpers
└── types/                      # page-level types
```

- Keep the route layer (`src/app`) thin. App Router files should mostly connect routes to feature pages and define metadata/layout when needed.
- Keep styling colocated: page styles beside the page file, component styles beside the component file.
- Keep copy and static data out of JSX when it is meaningful page content. Put it in that page's `constants/` folder.
- Empty folders are not required. Add `components`, `constants`, `services`, `hooks`, `utils`, or `types` only when the page actually needs them.
